/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  mixing01 -- THE OLD MIXING RULE BESIDE THE NEW ONE, AT MEASURED POINTS
  (DEV.md 4c C331).

  A mixture property is the pure values PLUS a rule that combines them, and
  the rule is a model with its own error.  Every operation below evaluates
  one property at one measured (T, x) with one rule, selected by
  `mixingRule` beside the pure-component `model`.  The measured value of
  each point is quoted here with its source; the run prints only what the
  engine computes.

  SURFACE TENSION, water + acetone, 298.15 K, sigma [mN/m]
  measured: Enders, Kahl & Winkelmann, J. Chem. Eng. Data (2007),
  doi 10.1021/je7000182.  Pure sigma: StoredCorrelation of each record.
     x_acetone   measured   moleFractionAverage     Butler (UNIFAC)
     0.0500       46.14       69.08  (+50 %)         31.93  (-31 %)
     0.1999       32.21       61.86  (+92 %)         27.52  (-15 %)
     0.4999       27.04       47.41  (+75 %)         25.81  ( -5 %)
     0.7999       24.51       32.95  (+34 %)         24.39  ( -0.5 %)
  The straight line ignores that acetone crowds the surface; Butler puts the
  surface in equilibrium with the bulk and recovers most of the drop, but
  with the case's own UNIFAC on BOTH phases it over-enriches the dilute end
  (Poling 5ed sec. 12-5: "none of these versions ... was particularly
  successful for aqueous mixtures"; Suarez et al. 1989 modify the
  combinatorial term for that reason, not done here).

  VISCOSITY, water + ethanol, 298.15 K, mu [mPa s]
  measured: Pires, Costa, Ferreira et al., J. Chem. Eng. Data (2007),
  doi 10.1021/je600565m.  Pure mu: Vogel of each record.
     x_ethanol   measured   logMoleAverage          GrunbergNissan
     0.1000       1.923       0.908  (-53 %)          1.558  (-19 %)
     0.3000       2.342       0.944  (-60 %)          2.460  ( +5 %)
     0.5984       1.761       1.001  (-43 %)          1.783  ( +1 %)
  The water-ethanol viscosity has a MAXIMUM near x_ethanol 0.25 that no
  average of the pure values can produce.  G(T, x) below is Choupo's fit to
  311 points from 9 laboratories (268-328 K); it is in-sample, and its
  largest error over those points is 27.5 % (mean 10.2 %).

  THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY, water + ethanol, lambda [W/(m K)]
  measured: Cui, Zhu, He, Wu et al., J. Chem. Eng. Data (2019),
  doi 10.1021/acs.jced.8b01147.  Pure lambda: chemsepEq16 of each record.
     T [K]   x_ethanol  measured  massFractionAverage  Filippov       Li
     293.26  0.2069     0.362     0.4245 (+17 %)       0.3499 (-3 %)  0.3393 (-6 %)
     293.16  0.4771     0.240     0.2951 (+23 %)       0.2298 (-4 %)  0.2288 (-5 %)

  Over every measured point of the archive (bin/curate/mixing_fit.py
  validate; max / mean |error| in %):
     sigma water-acetone (60 pts)    moleFractionAverage 104 / 63   Butler 33 / 12
     mu    water-ethanol  (311 pts)  logMoleAverage       72 / 42   GrunbergNissan 28 / 10
     k     water-ethanol  (117 pts)  massFractionAverage  28 / 17   Filippov 5 / 3   Li 10 / 6

  The golden master locks every computed value at reltol 1e-4.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        sigma_acetone0500_moleFractionAverage;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state       { T 298.15 K;  P 1 bar;  composition { water 0.9500; acetone 0.0500; } }
        thermo      { transport { interface { surfaceTension { model StoredCorrelation; mixingRule moleFractionAverage; } } } }
        properties  ( surface_tension );
    }
    {
        name        sigma_acetone0500_Butler;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state       { T 298.15 K;  P 1 bar;  composition { water 0.9500; acetone 0.0500; } }
        thermo      { transport { interface { surfaceTension { model StoredCorrelation; mixingRule Butler; } } } }
        properties  ( surface_tension );
